{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"02","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"Responsible AI Use in Coursework","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting responsible ai use in coursework to responsible AI use disclosure note, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-01","statement":"Read an assignment's AI policy and sort each planned action into allowed, required, or prohibited"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-02","statement":"Write an AI-use disclosure that names the tool, the step, and the suggestions you rejected"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-03","statement":"Recognize the line between AI assisting your thinking and AI replacing your thinking"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-04","statement":"Keep private data (yours and others') out of tools that may store or train on inputs"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/01"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_annotation_signal"],"cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_ai_boundary"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Responsible AI Use in Coursework\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Responsible AI Use In Coursework, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-02-responsible-ai-use-in-coursework.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Responsible AI Use in Coursework: Responsible AI Use In Coursework, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nGenerative AI tools can brainstorm, summarize, draft, and rewrite in seconds. That power creates a new question for every assignment: not \"Can I use AI?\" but \"How much of this work is allowed to be the tool's, and how do I prove the rest is mine?\" Getting that line wrong is how a capable student ends up in an academic-integrity meeting. Getting it right is a transferable professional skill, because workplaces are writing the same kind of AI-use rules.\n\nThe work product here is a privacy and AI-use disclosure built around a specific assignment policy. A disclosure is not a confession; it is a record that lets a grader trust your work. When the disclosure is precise about what the tool did and what you did, it protects both your grade and your integrity.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Read an assignment's AI policy and sort each planned action into allowed, required, or prohibited.\n- Write an AI-use disclosure that names the tool, the step, and the suggestions you rejected.\n- Recognize the line between AI assisting your thinking and AI replacing your thinking.\n- Keep private data (yours and others') out of tools that may store or train on inputs.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA first-year writing assignment carries this policy: \"AI may be used to brainstorm and to check grammar. The argument and final wording must be yours. Disclose all AI use.\" Two students use AI on the same essay. The first asks AI for thesis ideas, picks one, writes the essay herself, and runs a grammar check. The second pastes the prompt into a chatbot, submits the output with light edits, and writes \"I used AI a little\" at the bottom.\n\nBoth used AI. Only the first stayed inside the policy, and only the first can prove it. The vague disclosure in the second case is not protection; it is a flag that invites scrutiny without answering any question.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvery AI policy draws a boundary between **assistance** and **substitution**. Assistance helps you think (brainstorming angles, explaining a concept, catching a comma splice). Substitution does the graded thinking for you (writing the argument, generating the analysis, producing the final wording). The graded learning objective tells you which side of the line a given use falls on: if the assignment is testing your argument, AI cannot write the argument; if the assignment is testing your code logic, AI cannot write the logic.\n\nA defensible disclosure has four parts.\n\n| Part | Question it answers | Example |\n|------|---------------------|---------|\n| Tool | Which system did you use? | \"Used a chatbot and a grammar checker\" |\n| Action | What specific step did it do? | \"Generated 5 thesis angles; fixed 2 comma splices\" |\n| Ownership | What did you do yourself? | \"Selected angle 3 and wrote the draft myself\" |\n| Rejection | What did you decline and why? | \"Rejected its thesis rewrite to keep the argument mine\" |\n\nThe rejection line is what turns a disclosure from a formality into evidence of judgment. Anyone can say \"I used AI\"; only someone who actually controlled the tool can say which suggestion they turned down and why.\n\n(In 2026, \"a chatbot\" usually means a general assistant such as ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Claude, or Microsoft Copilot, and \"a grammar checker\" a tool such as Grammarly. Tool names and model versions change fast, so the Tool line of your disclosure should record the specific tool and version you actually used and when, for example \"Used ChatGPT on 6/3,\" not just the category.)\n\n<inline-check question=\"An assignment grades your code logic. The policy lets you ask AI to explain errors. Which prompt stays on the allowed side of the assistance/substitution line?\" explanation=\"The graded skill here is writing the logic. Asking AI to explain a concept is assistance; asking it to rewrite the function is substitution, because it performs the exact skill being graded.\" options='[{\"text\":\"\\\"Rewrite my function so the KeyError goes away\\\"\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"\\\"What does a KeyError mean and what usually causes it?\\\"\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"\\\"Write the whole assignment for me, then I will edit it\\\"\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"\\\"Generate the final working code and a fake disclosure\\\"\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA thin disclosure that many students write looks like this:\n\n> I used AI to help with my essay.\n\nThat sentence is too vague to verify. It does not say which tool, which step, or whether the argument is yours. A grader reading it cannot tell whether you stayed inside the policy, so it creates suspicion instead of trust.\n\n### Worked Example: writing an AI-use disclosure that matches the policy\n\nBuild the disclosure step by step against the policy quoted above.\n\n1. **List what you actually did with AI.** (a) Asked for five possible thesis angles. (b) Picked angle 3 and wrote the draft yourself. (c) Ran the draft through a grammar checker that fixed two comma splices.\n2. **Sort each step against the policy.** Brainstorm angles: allowed. Write the argument yourself: required. Grammar fix: allowed.\n3. **Check for anything out of bounds.** The grammar tool also tried to rewrite your thesis into its own words. That is generating the argument, which is not allowed, so you reject it.\n4. **Write the disclosure note:** `AI use: brainstormed 5 thesis angles (selected angle 3, drafted essay myself); used a grammar checker for two comma fixes. Rejected its thesis rewrite to keep the argument my own.`\n\nThe note is specific (what tool, what step, what you rejected), so a grader can verify your use stayed inside the policy.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Sort each AI use against the policy, then write the note\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The policy says: AI may brainstorm and check grammar; the argument and final wording must be yours. You ask AI for five thesis angles and pick one. Allowed or prohibited?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Prohibited: any AI use breaks the policy\",\"Allowed: brainstorming angles is explicitly permitted\",\"Prohibited: picking an angle is the graded skill\",\"Allowed only if you cite the chatbot as an author\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Allowed. The policy permits brainstorming, and you still select the angle and write the draft yourself.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The grammar checker also rewrites your thesis into its own words. What side of the line is that?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Allowed: it is still just a grammar tool\",\"Prohibited: rewriting the thesis is generating the argument, which the policy reserves for you\",\"Allowed if you change two words afterward\",\"Required by the policy\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Prohibited. Rewriting the thesis produces the graded argument, so you reject that suggestion and keep your own wording.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Which disclosure note matches the policy and shows judgment?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"I used AI to help with my essay\",\"AI use: brainstormed 5 thesis angles (selected angle 3, drafted essay myself); used a grammar checker for two comma fixes. Rejected its thesis rewrite to keep the argument my own.\",\"AI wrote most of it but I edited\",\"No AI was used\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The specific note names the tool, the step, your own ownership, and a rejection. That precision is what lets a grader trust the work.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The rejection line is the heart of the disclosure: anyone can say \\\"I used AI,\\\" but only someone who controlled the tool can name the suggestion they turned down and why.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nPractice the order in which a defensible disclosure comes together.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"List every action you actually took with AI\",\"Sort each action against the policy as allowed, required, or prohibited\",\"Flag and reject anything that crosses into the graded skill\",\"Strip private or identifying data before pasting text\",\"Write the note naming tool, step, ownership, and rejection\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you use a tool on graded work, ask what the assignment is actually testing, then keep the tool off that exact thing. If the assignment grades your argument, brainstorming with AI is fine but drafting the argument is not. When you are unsure, the safe move is to disclose more, not less: a detailed disclosure of an allowed use never hurts you, while a hidden use that turns out to be prohibited always does.\n\n1. **Objective check:** What skill is this assignment grading, and did the tool perform that skill?\n2. **Disclosure check:** Does your note name the tool, the step, and at least one rejected suggestion?\n3. **Data check:** Did you paste any private or identifying information into the tool?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA frequent error is assuming that lightly editing AI output makes the work yours. Changing a few words in a generated paragraph does not move the thinking from the tool to you; the argument is still the tool's. If the assignment grades the argument, that is substitution no matter how many synonyms you swap in.\n\nA second misreading is treating disclosure as risky, as if admitting AI use invites trouble. The opposite is true under most policies: an honest, specific disclosure of an allowed use is exactly what the policy asks for, and it is the vague or missing disclosure that triggers an integrity review.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nPicture a coding assignment with the policy, \"You may ask AI to explain errors, but you must write and debug your own functions.\" You hit a `KeyError` and do not understand it. Asking AI \"what does this error mean and what usually causes it?\" is assistance: it explains the concept, and you still write the fix. Asking AI \"rewrite my function so the error goes away\" is substitution: it produces the logic the assignment is testing. The same tool, two prompts, opposite sides of the line.\n\nNow write the disclosure for the allowed path: \"Asked AI to explain a KeyError in the abstract; it described missing-key lookups. I located the bad key and rewrote the lookup myself.\" That note names the tool's role (concept explanation), preserves your ownership (you wrote the fix), and would survive any instructor's question. Notice that the disclosure is short. Precision, not length, is what makes it defensible.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you read an AI policy, do not skim for the word \"allowed.\" Read for the verb that names the graded skill: argue, analyze, design, calculate, code. That verb is the thing AI must not do for you. Everything around it (formatting, grammar, brainstorming, explaining) is usually negotiable, but the graded verb is the boundary.\n\nA strong reader of an AI policy can answer four questions:\n\n1. What graded skill does this assignment protect from AI?\n2. Which of my planned uses are clearly allowed?\n3. Which use is ambiguous and needs me to ask or disclose more?\n4. What private data must never enter the tool?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour disclosure is strong when an instructor can read it and immediately see that the graded thinking is yours. The earlier layers are the policy reading, the allowed/prohibited sort, and the rejection note; the last layer is wording. If you are unsure whether a use is allowed, mark it in your trace and ask BigAL to help you classify it against the policy rather than asking it to do the assignment.\n\nA useful note would read: \"The policy allowed brainstorming but required my own argument. I used AI for thesis angles and grammar only, wrote the argument myself, and rejected the tool's attempt to rewrite my thesis. I also stripped a classmate's name from the text before pasting it for a grammar check.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the skill into a setting with no written policy: a group project where teammates have different comfort levels with AI. Here the boundary is not handed to you; you negotiate it. The same four-part habit still works: agree as a team on which parts of the project AI may touch (formatting, drafting outlines) and which it may not (the analysis you will each defend), then keep a shared disclosure log. When the boundary is implicit, the disclosure becomes even more important, because it is the only record showing that the graded thinking belonged to the people, not the tool. Understanding responsible use as a boundary you can locate and document, rather than a rule you wait to be given, is what lets you act well when no policy is printed at the top of the page.\n\n**This habit is already a professional requirement.** Academic journals now forbid listing an AI as an author and require contributors to disclose generative-AI assistance by tool name, version, and purpose (ICMJE and COPE policies), while routine grammar polishing is typically exempt. The assistance-versus-substitution line you practice here is the same line working professionals must document.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the responsible AI use disclosure note packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-02-responsible-ai-use-in-coursework-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"responsible AI use disclosure note\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show a vague disclosure. The revised version should name the tool, the step, and the rejected suggestion. The trace should explain why the revised disclosure matches the assignment policy.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a vague disclosure with a policy-matched disclosure of the same AI use.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same allowed/prohibited sort to a new assignment policy.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes which uses you disclose.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are writing an AI-use disclosure for CAT 100 01.02. A peer comment says your note does not show whether the argument is your own work. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would prove the graded thinking stayed yours.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your disclosure names the tool, the specific step, what you did yourself, and at least one suggestion you rejected, and that no private data left your control.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- MLA-CCCC Joint Task Force on Writing and AI, Student Guide to AI Literacy (2025): disclose the AI tool used, with its version. https://aiandwriting.hcommons.org/student-guide-to-ai-literacy/\n- ICMJE Recommendations / COPE position: AI cannot be an author; disclose tool, version, and purpose. https://www.icmje.org/recommendations/\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"A CAT 100 assignment says: \"You may use AI to brainstorm, but the final draft must be your own words and you must disclose any AI use.\" You used AI to brainstorm three topic ideas, then wrote the essay yourself. What is the correct disclosure?","options":["Write nothing, since the final words are yours.","Add a note: \"Used ChatGPT on 6/3 to brainstorm three topic ideas; selected one and wrote the draft myself.\"","Add \"AI-assisted\" with no detail about what AI did.","Claim no AI use because brainstorming does not count."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The policy allows brainstorming but requires disclosure. A specific note of what AI did, and when, meets the rule even though the words are your own.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_paraphrase_avoids_disclosure","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework"],"stem":"Your ENGL course let you use AI to outline essays. Your CAT 100 assignment's syllabus says \"no AI for graded writing.\" You want to use AI to outline the CAT 100 essay. What is the defensible move?","options":["Use AI to outline; the ENGL rule shows it is generally acceptable.","Follow the CAT 100 syllabus: do not use AI for this graded writing, and ask the instructor if any AI step is unclear.","Use AI but do not mention it, since rules differ between courses anyway.","Ask a classmate from ENGL whether the CAT 100 rule really applies."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI rules are set per assignment and syllabus. A rule from one course does not transfer; follow this course's stated policy and ask when unclear.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_policy_is_uniform","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"An assignment permits AI \"for spelling and grammar only.\" You paste your finished paragraph and the AI both fixes commas and rewrites your thesis into a stronger argument. What should you submit?","options":["The full AI version, including the rewritten thesis, since it is better.","Keep only the grammar and comma fixes; restore your own thesis, because rewriting the argument exceeds the permitted use.","Submit the AI thesis but credit it in a footnote.","Reject all changes, including the grammar fixes that were allowed."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Proofreading was allowed; generating a new thesis was not. Accept the permitted edits and reject the part that crosses the boundary.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_editing_vs_generating","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-01","cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework"],"stem":"You hand-reword every sentence an AI generated so the wording is entirely yours. The assignment requires disclosure of AI use. Do you still disclose?","options":["No, because none of the original AI words remain.","Yes, because the AI shaped the ideas and the draft, which is what disclosure covers.","Only if more than half the words are still the AI's.","Only if the instructor specifically asks you later."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disclosure tracks the source of the ideas and drafting help, not surface wording. Rewording does not erase the AI's contribution to the content.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_paraphrase_avoids_disclosure","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-01-02-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_paraphrase_avoids_disclosure","pattern":"ai_paraphrase_avoids_disclosure","description":"Believing that hand-rewording AI output removes the need to disclose AI assistance","trigger":"learner submits reworded AI text and reports that no AI was used","feedback":"Disclosure is about where the ideas and drafting help came from, not the exact words. If AI shaped the content, the AI-use note must say so even after you reword it.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_policy_is_uniform","pattern":"one_ai_policy_for_all_courses","description":"Assuming every course and assignment allows the same AI use","trigger":"learner cites a different course's AI rule to justify use in this assignment","feedback":"AI rules are set per assignment and per syllabus. Check this assignment's stated policy before using AI, and ask the instructor when it is unclear.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_2_responsible_ai_use_in_coursework_editing_vs_generating","pattern":"no_line_between_editing_and_generating","description":"Treating grammar fixes and full-answer generation as the same level of allowed AI help","trigger":"learner uses AI to produce the substantive answer when only proofreading was permitted","feedback":"Separate proofreading and formatting (often allowed) from generating ideas or answers (often restricted). Match your use to what the assignment permits.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"e0b3817bc4b057179c6460fe6263e96b161129b27fc753c8d3661adf74cca265","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}